  "response": "An unexpected product is assigned to a pathway not by weighing impressions but by forcing the stepwise and the concerted alternative to make divergent, quantitative predictions that can be tested one at a time. Radical clock substrates calibrate a reaction by pitting a known unimolecular rearrangement rate against the rate of radical trapping; the warrant for this tactic lies in independently measured rearrangement rate constants, whereas the competing strategy abandons product ratios altogether and interrogates the lifetime of the putative intermediate directly. The cyclopropylmethyl radical ring-opens with a rate constant of roughly 10^8 s^-1 at room temperature. Laser flash photolysis measures the lifetimes of transient intermediates on the nanosecond timescale. The experimentalist must therefore decide which of these two clocks the chemistry in question is fast enough to register.\n\nTEMPO traps carbon-centered radicals to give isolable alkoxyamine adducts. Radical inhibitors such as galvinoxyl and BHT suppress product formation in chain processes. TEMPO reacts only with carbocations and is inert toward carbon radicals, a description that cannot both hold together with the formation of isolable alkoxyamine adducts from carbon-centered radicals. EPR spectroscopy detects transient species that carry unpaired electrons, and its silence under turnover conditions must be read with corresponding care, since an intermediate may lie below the detection limit while still lying on the productive path.\n\nBecause Eyring analysis of temperature-dependent rate constants yields the entropy of activation for the reaction, it confirms that a strongly negative entropy of activation is consistent with a highly ordered single transition state. Entropic criteria carry weight precisely because they report on the organization of the rate-limiting step rather than on the fate of any species isolated afterward. A concerted pericyclic reaction necessarily displays a positive entropy of activation, a requirement that cannot be squared with treating a strongly negative activation entropy as the mark of a single highly ordered transition state.\n\nStereochemical probes supply the decisive complement to the kinetic ones, because the two pathways differ in whether configurational information survives the bond-forming event. Complete retention of stereochemistry at the reacting center is consistent with a concerted bond reorganization. Scrambling at that center indicates a freely rotating radical intermediate. By contrast with that scrambling diagnostic, the evidence assembled in the present case converges on a route in which the two new bonds form within a single transition state; that is, the product-forming step proceeds without any discrete intermediate. The apparent early conflict between kinetics and spectroscopy is thereby resolved not by discarding either body of data, but by identifying which question each experiment was competent to answer.",
